import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = '48 Hours in Cambria | 48 Hours in California';
const pageDescription =
  'A practical 48-hour Cambria itinerary with Moonstone Beach, coastal trails, village dining, local wine, where to stay, and options for extending your California getaway.';
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <article>
    <p>48-Hour California Getaway</p>

    <h1>48 Hours in Cambria</h1>

    <h2>
      Two days of ocean views, village wandering, coastal trails, local wine,
      and enough unscheduled time to actually enjoy being away.
    </h2>
    <img
      src="/images/Fiscalini_coast,_Cambria.jpg"
      alt="Rocky Pacific coastline at Fiscalini Ranch Preserve in Cambria, California"
      class="itinerary-hero"
    />
    <p>
      Cambria isn't a place that rewards rushing. This small Central Coast
      village sits between forested hills and the Pacific, with Moonstone Beach
      on one side and independent shops, restaurants, galleries, and tasting
      rooms on the other.
    </p>

    <p>
      This itinerary isn't designed to show you everything in Cambria. It's
      designed to help you have a really good two days there.
    </p>

    <h2>At a Glance</h2>

    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Couples, relaxed weekends, coastal scenery, food, and wine</p>
    <p><strong>Ideal trip:</strong> 2 nights / 48 hours</p>
    <p><strong>Home base:</strong> Moonstone Beach</p>
    <p><strong>Getting around:</strong> Car for the larger area; walking for parts of the village and coast</p>
    <p><strong>Best pace:</strong> One major outdoor experience, one wine tasting, and plenty of room to wander</p>
    <p><strong>Don't miss:</strong> Moonstone Beach, Fiscalini Ranch Preserve, and a taste of Cambria-grown wine</p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>
      For this itinerary, staying along Moonstone Beach Drive makes sense.
      You'll have the Pacific close by while remaining only a few minutes from
      Cambria's village.
    </p>

    <h3>Moonstone Landing — Best for Oceanfront Location</h3>

    <p>
      A locally owned property directly across from Moonstone Beach, this is a
      strong choice for travelers who want the coastline to be part of the stay
      rather than simply somewhere they visit.
    </p>

    <h3>White Water — Best for a Special Getaway</h3>

    <p>
      A more design-conscious boutique option with a relaxed Central Coast
      character, particularly suited to couples or travelers who want the hotel
      itself to feel like part of the getaway.
    </p>

       <h3>Castle Inn — Best for Practical Value</h3>

    <p>
      A straightforward Moonstone Beach option that keeps you close to the
      water without requiring the more upscale experience.
    </p>

    <h3>Check Cambria Hotel Availability</h3>

    <p>
      Compare current lodging options and availability for your travel dates.
    </p>

    <div class="eg-widget" data-widget="search" data-program="us-expedia" data-lobs="stays" data-network="pz" data-camref="1100l6uuC8" data-pubref=""></div>
    <script class="eg-widgets-script" src="https://creator.expediagroup.com/products/widgets/assets/eg-widgets.js"></script>

    <h2>Day 1 — Arrive, Unpack, and Slow Down</h2>

    <p>
      Aim to arrive in Cambria during the early afternoon. Check into your
      hotel, leave the luggage behind, and resist the temptation to immediately
      start checking attractions off a list.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instead, spend your first few hours getting acquainted with the village.
      Wander Main Street, browse locally owned shops and galleries, stop for
      coffee if something catches your eye, and leave some room for discovery.
    </p>

    <h3>Late Afternoon — Moonstone Beach</h3>

    <p>
      As the afternoon begins to wind down, head toward Moonstone Beach. Walk
      part or all of the coastal boardwalk, stop at the overlooks, and give
      yourself time to simply watch the Pacific.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no need to turn the walk into another task. The coastline is the
      experience.
    </p>

    <h3>Dinner — Robin's Restaurant</h3>

    <p>
      For the first evening, head into the village for dinner at Robin's
      Restaurant. Its relaxed atmosphere and globally influenced cooking make
      it a good arrival-night choice.
    </p>

    <p>
      After dinner, stop. Return to Moonstone Beach, enjoy your hotel, or simply
      call it an early evening. Tomorrow is the main Cambria day.
    </p>

    <h2>Day 2 — Trails, Cambria Flavors, and Coastal Wine</h2>

    <h3>Breakfast</h3>

    <p>
      If you're visiting on a weekend, start at The Hidden Kitchen for its
      distinctive blue-corn waffles and casual outdoor setting. Because
      operating days can vary, weekday travelers should check current hours and
      use a local breakfast alternative when needed.
    </p>

    <h3>Morning — Fiscalini Ranch Preserve</h3>

    <p>
      Spend the morning at Fiscalini Ranch Preserve, where coastal trails and
      bluff-top views provide one of Cambria's defining outdoor experiences.
    </p>

    <img
      src="/images/cambria-fiscalini-bluffs.jpg"
      alt="Bluffs and shoreline at Fiscalini Ranch Preserve in Cambria, California"
    />

    <p>
      This doesn't need to become an athletic achievement. Walk as far as feels
      right, watch for wildlife, use the benches, and stop when the scenery
      gives you a reason to stop.
    </p>

    <h3>Lunch — Linn's</h3>

    <p>
      Return to the village for lunch at Linn's. Cambria and the olallieberry
      have become closely associated, so this is an especially appropriate
      place to try a slice of olallieberry pie.
    </p>

    <h3>Afternoon — Cutruzzola Vineyards</h3>

    <p>
      Now make one wine stop — not three.
    </p>

    <p>
      Visit Cutruzzola Vineyards in Cambria. Its focus on cool-climate Riesling
      and Pinot Noir grown near the coast makes this tasting particularly well
      suited to a Cambria itinerary.
    </p>

    <p>
      Check current tasting-room hours and arrangements before your trip.
    </p>

    <h3>Take a Break</h3>

    <p>
      Go back to your hotel. Browse another shop. Have coffee. Read. Sit by the
      ocean.
    </p>

    <p>
      We've deliberately built some unscheduled time into the afternoon.
    </p>

    <h3>Late Afternoon — Back to Moonstone Beach</h3>

    <p>
      Return to the coast as the light begins to change. Walk another portion
      of Moonstone Beach or simply find a comfortable place to watch the
      Pacific.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/images/cambria-moonstone-beach.jpg"
      alt="Moonstone Beach along the coast in Cambria, California"
    />
    <h3>Dinner — Brydge</h3>

    <p>
      Make the second evening the more special dinner. Brydge offers the
      intimate, chef-driven meal that provides a nice contrast to the first
      night's more casual experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      Afterward, don't schedule anything else.
    </p>

    <h2>Departure Morning — Coffee, One Last Look, and Home</h2>

    <p>
      There's no need to turn departure morning into another packed itinerary.
    </p>

    <p>
      Stop at Cambria Coffee Company for coffee, then take one last look around
      the village or coastline before starting home.
    </p>

    <p>
      And if you aren't quite ready to leave? That's where our next two options
      come in.
    </p>

    <h2>If You Have 3 More Hours</h2>

    <h3>Visit Hearst Castle</h3>

    <p>
      Rather than sacrificing a large part of one of your two Cambria days to a
      timed attraction, add Hearst Castle when your schedule gives you a few
      more hours.
    </p>

    <p>
      Its location near San Simeon makes it a natural extension of a Cambria
      getaway while allowing Cambria itself to remain the purpose of the
      original 48 hours.
    </p>

    <p>
      Check current tour schedules and make reservations in advance when
      appropriate.
    </p>

    <h2>If You Have One More Day</h2>

    <h3>Follow Highway 1 North</h3>

    <p>
      Turn the additional day into a coastal drive. Head north toward San
      Simeon, stop at the Elephant Seal Vista Point, and continue toward Ragged
      Point as time, weather, and road conditions allow.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no prize for reaching a particular turnaround point. Stop when
      the scenery tells you to stop.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>A winter travel note:</strong> Highway 1 conditions deserve
      particular attention during the rainy season. Slides, debris,
      construction, flooding, or temporary closures can change what's
      practical along the coast. Check current Caltrans road conditions shortly
      before leaving Cambria rather than relying only on the itinerary you read
      when planning your trip.
    </p>

    <h2>Before You Go</h2>

    <p>
      Check restaurant and tasting-room hours shortly before traveling,
      particularly during the quieter winter season. Reserve the experiences
      that would seriously disappoint you to miss, but don't schedule every
      hour.
    </p>

    <p>
      Bring layers. Coastal weather can change considerably between morning,
      afternoon, and evening.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're planning to drive Highway 1, check current road conditions
      before setting out, especially during winter weather.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most importantly, leave some room in the itinerary for Cambria itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>
        The goal isn't to spend 48 hours checking things off a list. It's to
        come home feeling like you actually went somewhere.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <h2>Related Getaways</h2>

    <p>
        
      Continue exploring the Central Coast with
